Lack of social support
Social & Environmental
Absence of emergency contact, Difficulty managing ADLs independently, Low MSPSS score

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-019
Reason for Visit: Acute functional collapse and suspected severe infection in a profoundly isolated elderly patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage initiated by a neighbor who had not seen the patient in days. The patient was found on the floor, confused, and unable to manage basic needs. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute sepsis, stroke, or fracture, and to prevent further life-threatening physiological decline prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I fell two days ago, I couldn't reach the phone, and I have no one to call anyway."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 48-hour history of being unable to get up from the floor following a mechanical fall, complicated by a pervasive feeling of loneliness and isolation. Key symptoms include the complete absence of a designated emergency contact or caregiver. The patient demonstrates significant difficulty managing activities of daily living independently, which has worsened drastically since the fall. There is obvious non-adherence to complex medical regimens due to a lack of assistance. The patient exhibits depressed mood, severe dehydration, and poor nutritional status due to the inability to prepare meals. Functional decline is out of proportion to the initial minor physical trauma.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound/floor-bound, completely dependent
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme isolation, severe risk of pressure injuries, acute kidney injury from dehydration, high mortality risk if left unmonitored

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/55 | HR: 115 | Temp: 38.1 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Frail, unkempt, lying on the floor, dry mucous membranes, strong odor of urine, confused
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals severe tachycardia, postural hypotension (unable to stand for formal check), and weak peripheral pulses. Skin examination shows a stage 2 pressure injury developing on the sacrum and a resolving hematoma on the right hip without obvious shortening or external rotation. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: absolute absence of an emergency contact, acute inability to manage ADLs, and a highly precarious social support baseline. Abdomen is soft but bladder is palpable.
Environmental Observations: House is cold, spoiled food on the counter, phone cord unplugged, no personal alert system present.
Medication Reconciliation: Multiple medications scattered on the table; unable to determine compliance.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lack of social support (Acute functional decline with suspected urosepsis/dehydration) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Hip Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of leg shortening, external rotation, and the ability to bear some weight on the joint with assistance, though pain is present.
2. Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of focal neurological deficits (no facial droop, equal arm drift) and the profound global dehydration and confusion explaining the mental state.
3. Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear history of a mechanical fall, prolonged immobility, and objective signs pointing directly to hypovolemia and infection rather than isolated cardiac ischemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute, life-threatening physiological collapse—characterized by severe hypotension, tachycardia, pyrexia, and delirium—directly resulting from a prolonged lie after a fall in the setting of absolute social isolation. Deferring assessment until regular hours would have likely resulted in fatal septic shock or acute renal failure. The objective clinical signs of shock and infection completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention and necessitate immediate emergency medical retrieval for resuscitation and comprehensive multidisciplinary inpatient care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate application of warm blankets. Urgent dispatch of priority ambulance. Positioning the patient safely to prevent aspiration and further pressure injury pending retrieval.
Positive Behavioral Change: By wearing a personal emergency alarm pendant around your neck at all times once you return home, you ensure that a simple press of a button will summon immediate help if you ever fall again, preventing this prolonged suffering.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory referral to hospital social work and Aged Care Assessment Team (ACAT) for urgent high-level home care or residential aged care placement upon discharge.
DME/Safety (Optional): None acutely; requires complete environmental overhaul post-discharge.
Follow-up: Handover to Emergency Department medical team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate emergency transport to the nearest tertiary hospital via ambulance. Continuous monitoring of airway and conscious state while awaiting paramedic arrival.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute dehydration and infection require immediate intravenous fluids and antibiotics in the hospital. Discharging back to this isolated environment without massive support interventions is unsafe.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to sit up or drink water quickly, as you may choke. The ambulance is coming to take you to the hospital where they can safely give you fluids through a drip.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Lying on the floor for two days without water has damaged your kidneys and likely caused a severe urine infection. The hospital is the only safe place to treat this level of dehydration and confusion.
Actionable Steps: Rest quietly. Once in the hospital, you must allow the social workers to organize daily help for you at home, as it is no longer safe for you to live completely independently.

